steps.body:
An interface type lists method signatures. Any type that has all of those methods satisfies the interface automatically. There is no `implements` keyword. Go checks the methods at compile time wherever you use a value as the interface, so a missing method is a compile error, not a crash.

```go
type Shape interface {
	Area() float64
	Name() string
}
```

Once `Rect` and `Circle` both have `Area` and `Name`, either one can go wherever a `Shape` is expected, including a `[]Shape` that mixes them.

```go
shapes := []Shape{Rect{W: 3, H: 4}, Circle{R: 1}}
for _, s := range shapes {
	fmt.Println(s.Area())
}
```

Calling `s.Area()` runs the method of whatever concrete type `s` holds right now: `Rect.Area` for the rectangle, `Circle.Area` for the circle. The loop never needs to know which is which. Through a `Shape` you can only call the interface's own methods, so `s.W` does not compile even when `s` holds a `Rect`.

example.code:
package main

import (
	"fmt"
	"math"
)

type Shape interface {
	Area() float64
	Name() string
}

type Rect struct{ W, H float64 }

type Circle struct{ R float64 }

func (r Rect) Area() float64 { return r.W * r.H }
func (r Rect) Name() string  { return "rect" }

func (c Circle) Area() float64 { return math.Pi * c.R * c.R }
func (c Circle) Name() string  { return "circle" }

func main() {
	shapes := []Shape{Rect{W: 3, H: 4}, Circle{R: 1}, Circle{R: 2}}
	var total float64
	for _, s := range shapes {
		fmt.Printf("%s: %.2f\n", s.Name(), s.Area())
		total += s.Area()
	}
	fmt.Printf("Total area: %.2f\n", total)
}

