practice.solution:
package main

import "fmt"

type Animal interface {
	Sound() string
	Legs() int
}

type Dog struct{}

type Bird struct{}

type Fish struct{}

func (d Dog) Sound() string { return "Woof" }
func (d Dog) Legs() int     { return 4 }

func (b Bird) Sound() string { return "Tweet" }
func (b Bird) Legs() int     { return 2 }

func (f Fish) Sound() string { return "Blub" }
func (f Fish) Legs() int     { return 0 }

func main() {
	animals := []Animal{Dog{}, Bird{}, Fish{}}
	var total int
	for _, a := range animals {
		fmt.Printf("%s: %d legs\n", a.Sound(), a.Legs())
		total += a.Legs()
	}
	fmt.Printf("Total legs: %d\n", total)
}

steps.body:
A parameter of interface type accepts any value that satisfies it, so one function works for many types. Go code prefers small interfaces with one or two methods, like `Notifier` here or `fmt.Stringer` in the standard library, declared next to the code that uses them.

```go
func broadcast(ns []Notifier, msg string) {
	for _, n := range ns {
		fmt.Println(n.Notify(msg))
	}
}
```

**Pointer receivers matter.** If `Notify` is declared on `*SMS`, only `*SMS` has that method, so you must store `&sms`. Writing `sms` fails to compile with "Notify method has pointer receiver". The automatic `&` that `c.Inc()` gets on a variable never happens when a value is stored in an interface. Value-receiver methods work for both `Email` and `*Email`.

In memory an interface value is two words: one points at the concrete type, the other at the data. Storing a struct value copies the struct, so later changes to the original do not show through the interface. Storing `&sms` copies only the pointer, so every call updates the same `sms`. `%T` prints the concrete type an interface holds.

example.code:
package main

import "fmt"

type Notifier interface {
	Notify(msg string) string
}

type Email struct{ Addr string }

type SMS struct {
	Phone string
	Sent  int
}

func (e Email) Notify(msg string) string {
	return fmt.Sprintf("email to %s: %s", e.Addr, msg)
}

func (s *SMS) Notify(msg string) string {
	s.Sent++ // changes the SMS that the pointer points at
	return fmt.Sprintf("sms to %s: %s", s.Phone, msg)
}

func broadcast(ns []Notifier, msg string) {
	for _, n := range ns {
		fmt.Println(n.Notify(msg))
	}
}

func main() {
	sms := SMS{Phone: "555-0101"}
	ns := []Notifier{Email{Addr: "ada@example.com"}, &sms}
	fmt.Printf("%T %T\n", ns[0], ns[1])
	broadcast(ns, "build passed")
	broadcast(ns, "deploy done")
	fmt.Printf("texts sent: %d\n", sms.Sent)
}

